```php
function countWeekendDays(string $start, string $end): int
{
    $s = strtotime($start);
    $e = strtotime($end);
    if ($s === false || $e === false || $s > $e) {
        return 0;
    }

    $days = intdiv($e - $s, 86400) + 1;
    $fullWeeks = intdiv($days, 7);
    $weekends = $fullWeeks * 2;

    $remainder = $days % 7;
    if ($remainder > 0) {
        $startDow = (int)date('N', $s); // 1=Mon, 7=Sun
        for ($i = 0; $i < $remainder; $i++) {
            $dow = ($startDow + $i - 1) % 7 + 1;
            if ($dow >= 6) {
                $weekends++;
            }
        }
    }

    return $weekends;
}
```

This O(1) solution avoids iterating through every day. It calculates full weeks (each contributing exactly 2 weekend days) and only loops through the remaining 0–6 days. Using `date('N')` provides ISO day-of-week numbering (1–7), making Saturday/Sunday detection a simple `>= 6` check. The function handles invalid inputs gracefully and assumes inclusive date ranges.

Can you spot the critical bug in this javascript code?

```js
function reloadResource(){
    fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
        method: 'GET',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        setInterval(reloadResource, 1000);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}
```

The critical bug is calling `setInterval(reloadResource, 1000)` inside the success callback. Each successful fetch spawns a *new* recurring interval without clearing previous ones. This causes exponential growth in concurrent requests, quickly overwhelming the browser, network, and server.

Additionally, if a request takes longer than 1 second, multiple intervals will stack up even faster, as each completion triggers another `setInterval`.

**Fix:** Use `setTimeout` for recursive polling, or move `setInterval` outside the function entirely.

**Corrected recursive approach:**
```js
function reloadResource() {
    fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
        method: 'GET',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        setTimeout(reloadResource, 1000); // Schedules next call only after current completes
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
        setTimeout(reloadResource, 1000); // Optionally retry on error
    });
}

reloadResource(); // Initial call
```

`setTimeout` ensures only one pending request exists at a time and prevents interval accumulation. Also note: `Content-Type` header is unnecessary for GET requests (it describes request bodies, which GET typically lacks).

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

pub trait Observer<T> {
    fn next(&self, value: T);
    fn complete(&self);
}

pub struct Subject<T> {
    observers: Arc<Mutex<Vec<Box<dyn Observer<T> + Send + Sync>>>>,
}

impl<T: Clone + Send + 'static> Subject<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            observers: Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new())),
        }
    }

    pub fn subscribe<O: Observer<T> + Send + Sync + 'static>(&self, observer: O) {
        self.observers.lock().unwrap().push(Box::new(observer));
    }

    pub fn next(&self, value: T) {
        let observers = self.observers.lock().unwrap();
        for obs in observers.iter() {
            obs.next(value.clone());
        }
    }

    pub fn complete(&self) {
        let observers = self.observers.lock().unwrap();
        for obs in observers.iter() {
            obs.complete();
        }
    }
}

// Usage example
struct PrintObserver;
impl Observer<i32> for PrintObserver {
    fn next(&self, v: i32) { println!("Received: {}", v); }
    fn complete(&self) { println!("Stream completed"); }
}
```

